<header><p class="st-kicker">city state · Anatolia and the Levant</p><h1 class="st-title">Phaselis</h1><p class="st-meta">690 BCE – 77 BCE (approx.) · capital Phaselis</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Phaselis, a Rhodian colony with three harbours on the Lycian coast, a trading city later held by pirates and taken by Rome.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Colonists from <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/lindos">Lindos</a> on <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/rhodes">Rhodes</a> founded Phaselis, traditionally in 690 BCE, on a peninsula at the border of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/lycian-dynasts">Lycia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pamphylia">Pamphylia</a>. Its position on the coastal route between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aegean">Aegean</a> and the eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> made it a natural port of call.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Phaselis was a Greek city governed by its citizens and a member of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/delian-league">Delian League</a>. It traded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timber">timber</a>, rose oil and other goods and had a share in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hellenion">Hellenion</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naucratis">Naukratis</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>. Alexander of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/macedonian-empire">Macedonian Empire</a> stayed there in the winter of 334/333 BCE.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In the 1st century BCE the pirate leader <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zenicetes">Zenicetes</a> held Phaselis. The Roman general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/publius-servilius-vatia-isauricus">Publius Servilius Isauricus</a> captured it in 77 BCE, and the city was then absorbed into the Roman world, later joining the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/lycian-league">Lycian League</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/phaselis.jpg" alt="Phaselis" width="320" height="219"><figcaption>Image: Alexander Buschorn at de.wikipedia · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Phaselis_axb01.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>690 BCE – 77 BCE (approx.)</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>city state</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>mercantile</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Phaselis</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Greek religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Greek</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Turkey</dd></dl></aside></div>

